detailedExplanation.howItWorks:
ZORDER is invoked as part of OPTIMIZE; Delta sorts rows so that the chosen columns are interleaved, clustering similar values together so each file's min/max range is narrow and many files can be skipped. Liquid clustering is set on the table and maintained over time.

```sql
-- ZORDER: applied during OPTIMIZE, fixed column choice
OPTIMIZE events ZORDER BY (user_id, event_type);

-- Liquid clustering: declared once, maintained automatically
CREATE TABLE events (...) CLUSTER BY (user_id, event_type);
-- change keys later without a full rewrite
ALTER TABLE events CLUSTER BY (user_id, country);
```

With liquid clustering, writes and OPTIMIZE keep the data clustered incrementally, so you avoid the all-or-nothing rewrites ZORDER forces, and you can evolve the keys as query patterns shift.

On write, Delta computes min/max/null stats per file and stores them in the log. On read, the optimizer evaluates the filter against each file's stats and prunes files whose ranges cannot match.

```sql
-- Stats are collected for the first 32 columns by default;
-- you can change how many columns get stats
ALTER TABLE events SET TBLPROPERTIES (
  'delta.dataSkippingNumIndexedCols' = '8'
);

-- A selective filter lets Delta skip non-matching files
SELECT * FROM events WHERE event_date = '2024-01-01';
```

Skipping only helps when the laid-out data has narrow, non-overlapping ranges per file for the filtered column, which is why you cluster on the columns you filter on. Putting wide, high-cardinality string columns first in the stats also wastes stat budget, so column order and the indexed-column count matter.

You diagnose the bottleneck in the Spark UI (spill points to memory, low core utilization to too many workers, long shuffles to network) and choose instance type and count accordingly, then enable autoscaling with bounds and a spot strategy.

```json
{
  "autoscale": { "min_workers": 2, "max_workers": 12 },
  "node_type_id": "<memory-optimized-for-shuffle-heavy>",
  "aws_attributes": { "availability": "SPOT_WITH_FALLBACK",
                       "first_on_demand": 1 }
}
```

Keep the driver on-demand, allow spot for the bulk of workers with fallback to on-demand if spot is unavailable, and bound autoscaling so it scales for big stages but returns to a small floor when idle.

VACUUM finds files marked removed in the log that are older than the retention threshold and deletes them; it does not touch files still referenced by the current version.

```sql
-- Remove obsolete files older than the default 7-day retention
VACUUM sales;

-- Preview what would be deleted without deleting
VACUUM sales DRY RUN;

-- Custom retention (be careful: shrinks the time-travel/safety window)
VACUUM sales RETAIN 168 HOURS;
```

The retention threshold is the key knob: a shorter window reclaims storage sooner but reduces how far back you can time travel and risks deleting files an in-progress long query still reads, which is why Databricks guards against very short retention by default.
